// spec: DCL-006, INI-003, CLS-005
//
// A class may inherit from one a module declares -- and whether it can turns
// on WHERE the 'uses' stands, because a module runs at its clause [INI-003].
//
// ⚠️ This is the limit of hoisting, and the reason it has one. A class is
// built ahead of the file's statements only when its parent is absent or is
// another class of the SAME file; one inheriting from anything else is built
// where its declaration stands. So the parent has to have been imported by
// then -- hoisting the build too would need the superclass evaluated before
// the 'uses' that supplies it.

uses 'modules/Shape';

WriteLn ('root, after the import');

class Circle (Shape);
begin
    constructor Init (); begin end
end

WriteLn (Circle ().Name ());
WriteLn (Circle () is Shape);

// A class of this file may still be inherited from either side of its own
// declaration, which is what hoisting is for.
class Puppy (Hound);
begin
    constructor Init (); begin end
end

class Hound;
begin
    constructor Init (); begin end
    function Name (); begin Exit 'hound'; end
end

WriteLn (Puppy ().Name ());
